I've written the five-year durability ("future") review of the Grace Reiter draft to `review-future.json`. It passes the schema check, its two content fingerprints match the request, and every quoted passage matches the draft exactly. I didn't touch any other file or publish anything.

**Verdict: the profile mostly holds up over five years.** Take out the latest news (SNL casting, the Netflix show, the unreleased Twilight parody) and the argument still stands. It opens on her May 2025 podcast confession, not the SNL news, and the case rests on dated 2025–2026 statements plus three people who describe the same thing. Most facts that will change are dated and have a clear trigger for when to recheck them.

**Two major findings:**
- **The closing prediction would flip the type for the wrong reason.** It says that if a season of applause teaches her to accept praise, she's a Type 3. But she was already getting used to praise in April 2026: her roommate said "you got to get used to it" (the draft cuts that line), and she replied "I feel like I'm good at it now." Anyone gets more comfortable with praise between 25 and 30, so a later update could switch her to Type 3 just because she grew up. The review asks for a test that simply getting used to praise wouldn't pass, and for dates on the pattern so the description stays true even if the habit fades.
- **The Minimum Wage FAQ uses the future tense.** It says the show "premieres on October 26, 2026." The draft is unpublished and still has editing and fact-checking ahead, so it will probably go live after that date and be wrong from day one.

**Five minor findings:**
- The "clearest test" (whether she credits her castmates after a hard SNL week) mixes up type with normal press manners, since every SNL cast member credits castmates in interviews. It also assumes she'll still be on SNL.
- "now performs live on network television" and the FAQ's "At 25 she is…" will go out of date.
- "mid-October" has no year.
- "about four years" of evidence is vague and hides that nearly all of the deciding evidence is from 2025–2026, the year her career took off.
- The 2022 Passion Pod interview links to a raw audio file. I found its Apple Podcasts episode page and put the URL in the finding.

The similarity check was triggered by the phrase "core emotion," which is standard Enneagram vocabulary. The Inde Navarrette comparison shares only a general move, not the argument, so I didn't report either as a finding. I also protected five passages, including the opening and the Drew Afualo "you will believe it" closing moment.
